Problem Definition: The Data Integration Crisis	New Idea: From Data Chaos to Contractual Clarity
What are you trying to do? <br> We are solving the fundamental data integration problem that cripples real-time situational awareness on LEO satellites. Our goal is to create a single, trustworthy source of truth for a satellite's thermal-fluid health. <br><br> How is it done today? <br> Data is collected in isolated silos. A satellite's own sensors, intelligence from drones, and ground station telemetry are not harmonized. Analysis is performed manually, on the ground, long after the data is collected. There is no unified, real-time picture of the system's state. <br><br> What are the limits of current practice? <br> This data fragmentation makes robust autonomy impossible. You cannot build a reliable "battle management system" on a foundation of conflicting or incomplete data. This creates a critical vulnerability, as the satellite is flying blind until a human on the ground intervenes. <br><br> How is it related to the seedling solicitation? <br> Making a "dent" in LEO survivability begins with solving the data problem. Our proposal to harmonize and streamline this emerging data chaos is the essential first step toward building the truly resilient, autonomous systems the solicitation demands.


New Idea: From Data Chaos to Contractual Clarity
What is your idea? <br> We propose a two-stage solution: <br> 1. Data Integration via Contract-Based Design: We use a heterogeneous family of formal contracts as a universal language to define and verify expected behavior. All incoming data, regardless of its source, is checked against these contracts to create a single, coherent, and trustworthy view of the thermal system. <br> 2. Autonomous Decision Support: This contract-verified data stream then feeds an onboard decision support engine, enabling autonomous battle management for the satellite's thermal health. <br><br> What makes it novel? <br> We are flipping the script on contract-based design: it's not just a verification tool, but a powerful runtime data integration and sense-making engine. Injecting LLMs to generate these contracts from natural language makes this sophisticated approach practical and scalable. <br><br> What makes it more valuable than current practice? <br> It creates the foundational layer for true autonomy. Instead of just collecting data, we create understanding. This allows the satellite to reason about its own state and provides the reliability needed for a system to manage itself under fire.

Impact: Enabling Autonomous Battle Management	Path Forward
What CRAD agencies will be interested in this idea and why? <br> - Space Force / DARPA: This directly supports the vision for autonomous battle management and resilient space assets. Our framework provides the "brain" for a satellite to manage its own health, allowing it to "fight through" thermal attacks or anomalies without ground support. <br> - NASA: This framework solves a major challenge for complex, multi-instrument science missions by enabling the autonomous fusion and validation of data from various sources to ensure mission objectives are met even when anomalies occur. <br><br> If this is developed & applied, what qualitative difference will it make? <br> It elevates a satellite from a remote-controlled sensor platform to a self-aware, autonomous asset capable of independent action to ensure its survival. The paradigm shifts from reactive ground control to proactive, onboard battle management. <br><br> Quantitative benefit to CRAD customer, to RTX customer, and to RTX? <br> - CRAD Customer: Enables development of survivable satellite constellations with significantly reduced operational overhead. <br> - RTX Customer: Delivers a critical "survivability" and "autonomy" discriminator, increasing mission assurance in contested environments. <br> - RTX: Positions RTX as the leader in the foundational technology for next-generation, AI-driven resilient space systems.

Path Forward
Seedling (5 months/$120k) <br> Milestone 1 (Midterm): <br> - Data Integration Demo: Demonstrate the harmonization of at least two heterogeneous data streams (e.g., simulated onboard sensor data vs. a ground-based thermal model) against a single, LLM-generated thermal contract. <br> - Deliver the initial prototype application framework. <br> Milestone 2 (Final "Exam"): <br> - Autonomous Decision Support Demo: Deliver verification artifacts showing the end-to-end flow: integrated data streams trigger a verifiable contract violation, which in turn prompts a correct recommendation from the autonomous decision support engine in a simulated "battle management" scenario. <br><br> What is the CRAD marketing strategy? <br> We will market this as the "autonomy-ready" data backbone for resilient space systems. The prototype will be used to engage PMs at Space Force and DARPA working on autonomous systems, JADC2, and satellite survivability. <br><br> Identify partners required for follow-on CRAD teaming: <br> - Universities: Experts in data fusion, formal methods, and AI (e.g., MIT, Stanford). <br> - National Labs: AFRL (Space Vehicles Directorate), JPL (Autonomous Systems). <br> - Other Businesses: Satellite bus providers and sensor payload companies to provide realistic integration test cases.




